# EigenScript gap: a string is a value with no object identity, so Lua's
# lua_topointer of a string (string.format("%p", s), manual 6.4.1: "a unique
# string identifier") cannot tell one long string from an equal copy made
# elsewhere. EigenLua's direct translation names the value: equal strings get
# one id (src/rt_format.eigs lr_fx_strptr), so a string formats alike every
# time (Lua agrees), and an equal long string built by concatenation formats
# like the original (Lua gives it its own address). Not a #1286 row.
# Lua:  local s1 = string.rep("0123456789", 5)
#       local sd = "0123456789" .. string.rep("0123456789", 4)
#       print(string.format("%p", s1) ~= string.format("%p", sd)) -> true
# Expected: an identity (address or id) for a string value, or a builtin
# telling one string from an equal copy.
# Actual 686d3b7d: equal strings are indistinguishable, and the builtin index
# has no identity/address entry:
#   eigenscript --api | grep -ciE 'address|ptr|id_of|same_object'  ->  0
# Rows: literals.lua (230: getadd(sd) ~= a1), S.
a is "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"
b is "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa" + "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"
print of (a == b)
print of (type of a)
